    "case_id": "102",
    "case_summary": "A 72-year-old male patient presented with right flank pain and significant hematuria accompanied by clot retention. The patient had a history of heavy smoking but no history of trauma, recent medical procedures, or known kidney stones. He reported no bleeding disorders and was not on any medications. His blood pressure, blood count, biochemical, and coagulation parameters were all within normal limits. A rinsing catheter was used, and the hematuria resolved within a few days. Ultrasonography of the kidneys and bladder showed no abnormalities. A subsequent CT scan revealed a small soft tissue mass within the right renal pelvis, which did not show significant enhancement after contrast medium injection, and there was no dilation of the renal unit. No urinary tract stones or other pathologies were observed. The CT scan could not distinguish between a blood clot and a tumor. Despite the presence of a filling defect in the renal pelvis, which showed slight enhancement, the possibility of an urothelial lesion was considered. Urine cytology results were negative. A cystoscopy with the insertion of a ureteral catheter into the right pelvis was performed to selectively obtain a urine sample for cytology and conduct a retrograde study. Both cystoscopy and upper tract cytology were negative for high-grade bladder tumors. The retrograde study of the right ureter and pelvicaliceal system showed no filling defect in the right renal pelvis and calyces. Based on these findings, the CT findings were attributed to a blood clot in the right renal pelvis, and further investigation proceeded.\n\nSelective right renal arteriography demonstrated an area of tortuous, coiled vascular channels with early filling of the renal vein within two seconds after the start of the injection.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
